# Appendix: physical forcing of the columns

The biological model evolves inside a prescribed physical environment.
Light controls photosynthesis, and vertical mixing transports nutrients and
plankton through the water column. This appendix makes those inputs
inspectable alongside the biological experiments.

## Current reference column: idealised seasonal forcing

The default reference simulation uses a 300 m column with 30 uniform 10 m cells,
a 30-minute time step and vertically implicit mixing. Its
annual forcing begins in midwinter and repeats every 365 days. The
reference tutorial spans the complete annual cycle, including spring, summer,
autumn and winter.

WORKSHOP_PHYSICS_FIGURE

The figure shows three related physical inputs:

- **Surface PAR:** An idealised annual cycle, with a winter minimum and
  stronger illumination toward midsummer. The prescribed value is in W m⁻².
- **Mixed-layer depth:** A smooth, GOSML-informed seasonal transition from
  a roughly 95 m late-winter mixed layer toward a roughly 15 m summer layer.
- **Vertical diffusivity:** Stronger mixing above the mixed-layer base and
  weaker mixing below it. The white curve marks the prescribed mixed-layer
  depth in the time-depth diagram.

These are the same functions imported from `MASTSWorkshop` in the reference
exercise: `surface_PAR(t)`, `mixed_layer_depth(t)` and
`vertical_diffusivity(z, t)`. The water column uses the prescribed
vertical diffusivity as its closure, and LOBSTER calculates the attenuation
of PAR within the column.

## Idealised BATS and NABE comparison

The [BATS-NABE comparison](07_bats_nabe.md) adds a North Atlantic spring-bloom
scenario and uses the same stretched grid at both stations. The default
production exercises at BATS and NABE both use 300 m / 30-cell columns. Their `deep=true` alternatives use the shared 1200 m / 60-cell grid.

| Setting | Deep BATS | Idealised NABE |
|:--|:--|:--|
| Location represented | 31.7 N, 64.2 W | ~47 N, 20 W |
| Late-winter maximum mixed-layer depth | ~95 m | ~190 m (February) |
| Summer minimum mixed-layer depth | ~15 m | ~20 m |
| Surface PAR | 2-122 W/m2 | 2-98 W/m2 |
| Spring mixed-layer transition | Gradual shoaling March–May | Faster shoaling March–May |
| Initial near-surface nitrate | ~0.7 mmol N/m3 | ~6.0 mmol N/m3 |
| Vertical diffusivity, surface mixed layer | up to ~5.1e-3 m2/s | up to ~1.01e-2 m2/s |

### Seasonal mixing through the water column

WORKSHOP_STATION_DIFFUSIVITY_FIGURE

The panels show the prescribed vertical diffusivity at cell centres through
one idealised year, using a common **logarithmic colour scale** (m² s⁻¹).
The upper 500 m of the shared 1200 m deep grid is shown, where the seasonal
mixed layers affect nutrient resupply. White dashed curves mark the prescribed
mixed-layer depths; the horizontal dotted line indicates the 300 m teaching
domain. Below the mixed layer the background diffusivity is approximately
10⁻⁴ m² s⁻¹. NABE now peaks near 190 m in late winter, compared with
roughly 95 m at BATS; its seasonal shoaling remains more pronounced.

### Observational mixed-layer depth: NOAA GOSML

WORKSHOP_GOSML_COMPARISON_FIGURE

The two stations share the same **positive-down** observational depth convention
and vertical axis. **Blue circles** show only the GOSML monthly means, overlaid
on the **red daily idealised-forcing curves**. The original observational CSV
retains medians and percentiles for more detailed investigation. Both
idealised seasonal curves were revised using the observed monthly-mean comparison:
BATS peaks near 95 m and NABE near 190 m, with a later NABE winter maximum.

The quantitative comparison uses the **average of the idealised daily MLD across
each calendar month**. A positive bias means the model prescribes deeper mixing
than the observed monthly means. The table is regenerated automatically from the
committed CSV during each full documentation build.

WORKSHOP_GOSML_SUMMARY_TABLE

The observed and idealised curves use a common January-to-December calendar,
with day zero corresponding to 1 January. The idealised annual cycle represents
midwinter on day zero, while the monthly observations aggregate multiple years.
The forcing adjustment improves the match in seasonal depth and timing while
preserving a smooth idealised annual cycle. It changes simulated nutrient supply
and bloom timing, so annual ecosystem figures should be regenerated with a full
scientific docs build and assessed alongside spin-up sensitivity. Nitrate and
biomass also depend on the specified initial conditions.

These parameters are **first-pass teaching scenarios**, chosen to distinguish
subtropical conditions and a bloom-forming temperate Atlantic environment.
The plots labelled "top 10 m" use the uppermost model cell, occupying
0–10 m with its centre at **5 m depth** (z = −5 m); they show neither a
surface boundary value nor an upper-100 m average. NABE starts near
6 mmol N m⁻³ nitrate in this cell, compared with approximately
0.7 mmol N m⁻³ at BATS. During the first simulated year, deep winter mixing
can resupply NABE's surface layer from the more nitrate-rich water below.
The simulated spring bloom remains a provisional first-year outcome; its
magnitude and timing should be reassessed after the forcing update. The first
year includes adjustment from the specified initial profiles; comparison with
observed nutrients and biomass and multi-year spin-up tests will determine
how representative the bloom is.

The nitrate profiles are depth-dependent but are not yet calibrated against
station observations. Initial plankton and organic-matter fields also decrease
with depth to limit artificial deep biological inventories. The annual cycle begins in midwinter.

The shared deep domain reaches 1200 m with 60 cells, preserving 10 m resolution
through the upper 300 m, then increasing to 20 m and 40 m cells below 300 m
and 600 m. Export can be diagnosed at grid faces 100, 300, and 1000 m. All
vertical inventories use the physical thickness of each cell. The revised
idealised winter mixed layers remain within the 300 m teaching domain at
both stations. The deep simulation resolves export below the mixed layer
and the deeper nutrient reservoir.

### Scientific sources and next validation

- [OceanBioME idealised North Atlantic column](https://oceanbiome.github.io/OceanBioME.jl/stable/generated/column/):
  precedent for deep winter mixing, rapid spring shoaling and seasonal PAR.
- [NOAA World Ocean Atlas 2023](https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/access/world-ocean-atlas-2023/):
  source for the planned station-specific nitrate and temperature climatologies.
- [U.S. JGOFS NABE field observations](https://www.bco-dmo.org/dataset/2574/description):
  trace-metal and water-column measurements from the 1989 field campaign.

The present LOBSTER column is forced by prescribed light and vertical mixing;
temperature is not passed as a separate prognostic tracer or remineralisation
forcing. Temperature-dependent remineralisation is a candidate follow-up
experiment once the default column behaviour has been benchmarked.

Return to the [reference column](00_reference_column.md) or continue with the
[grazing experiment](01_grazing.md).
